      "seed": 1114768097,
      "version": 6175,
      "versionNonce": 1161199560,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1772502236124,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": " - Note: Break datasets into training and test/evaluation sets\nwith ratio of 70-20-10 or 70-15-15. Make sure to stratify the\nrecords so that it's presentative across all sets.\n\nPotential list of tables in the database:\n\n- Table 1: User profile. Store username, hashed password so they\ncan login from the UI later again using this credential. Other\nprofiles related features such as  preferences as vector or json\nformat, etc. Primary key: user_id\n\n- Table 2: Combined recipe records. We shall use a column name\ntype to assign train, test, eval for each record. Primary key:\nrecipe_id. Think about using JSONB format to store cooking\ninstruction if necessary.\n\n- Table 3: one to many table for storing recipe and its image file\nURLs. Foreign key: recipe_id\n\n- Table 4: similar to table 2, but for storing user reviews. Primary\nkey: review_id, foreign key: user_id. New reviews/rating from web\nUI will also be recorded here.\n\nTable 5,6,7 are optional if the cleaned grocery dataset is small,\nthen it's still fine to use as csv and load directly from memory for\nEDA and training.\n\n- Table 5: Grocery orders. Storing the history orders by user id\n(this is not the same user profile id in table 1, just for grouping\norders)\n\n- Table 6: Each order items, store as product_id vectors \n\n- Table 7: Store product_id and product_name, etc. for reference.\n\n- Table 8: Creating the inverted index - posting for each\ningredient with statistics such as Term frequency, average\nnutrient, quantity, etc. This table will be used for generating\nrecommendation candidates\n\n... ",
      "fontSize": 26.2916798942023,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "GACGVV9DSRX7NFGTMjmW0",
      "originalText": " - Note: Break datasets into training and test/evaluation sets with ratio of 70-20-10 or 70-15-15. Make sure to stratify the records so that it's presentative across all sets.\n\nPotential list of tables in the database:\n\n- Table 1: User profile. Store username, hashed password so they can login from the UI later again using this credential. Other profiles related features such as  preferences as vector or json format, etc. Primary key: user_id\n\n- Table 2: Combined recipe records. We shall use a column name type to assign train, test, eval for each record. Primary key: recipe_id. Think about using JSONB format to store cooking instruction if necessary.\n\n- Table 3: one to many table for storing recipe and its image file URLs. Foreign key: recipe_id\n\n- Table 4: similar to table 2, but for storing user reviews. Primary key: review_id, foreign key: user_id. New reviews/rating from web UI will also be recorded here.\n\nTable 5,6,7 are optional if the cleaned grocery dataset is small, then it's still fine to use as csv and load directly from memory for EDA and training.\n\n- Table 5: Grocery orders. Storing the history orders by user id (this is not the same user profile id in table 1, just for grouping orders)\n\n- Table 6: Each order items, store as product_id vectors \n\n- Table 7: Store product_id and product_name, etc. for reference.\n\n- Table 8: Creating the inverted index - posting for each ingredient with statistics such as Term frequency, average nutrient, quantity, etc. This table will be used for generating recommendation candidates\n\n... ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
